issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if (ret == AOF_OPEN_ERR || ret == AOF_FAILED || ret == AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED) {
    goto cleanup;
}

findings.body:
In the base-file branch, the PR adds `AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED` to the condition that jumps to `cleanup`:

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1829-1843"}

If the base file is not the last file, `ret` has already been changed to `AOF_FAILED` a few lines earlier. So the new clause only matters when the base file **is** the last file and was recovered. In that case, the jump skips the lines that run after the INCR loop:

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1880-1891"}

After a successful recovery, `server.aof_current_size` and `server.aof_rewrite_base_size` both stay at 0. `INFO persistence` reports wrong sizes. Auto-rewrite in `serverCron` falls back to `base = 1` (src/server.c:1588-1590), so the growth percentage becomes huge and a rewrite fires as soon as 64MB of new writes accumulate, however large the real AOF is. The INCR loop doesn't have this clause, so base and incr recovery behave differently.

The test "Corrupted base AOF (last file): should recover" doesn't catch this because it never checks INFO.

::suggestion{file="src/aof.c" lines="1841-1843"}
        if (ret == AOF_OPEN_ERR || ret == AOF_FAILED) {
            goto cleanup;
        }

The `uxeof` and normal-EOF paths handle a transaction cut off partway through: they rewind to `valid_before_multi` so the file never ends with an unclosed `MULTI`. The new `fmterr` branch always truncates at `valid_up_to`. That offset is updated after each *queued* command inside a MULTI, so it can point into the middle of a transaction:

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1724-1746"}

Scenario: the AOF ends with `MULTI`, `SET a 1`, then garbage. Recovery truncates right after `SET a 1`. Memory is consistent because the queued commands are dropped when the fake client is freed. But the file now ends with an open `MULTI`, and Redis starts appending new writes after it. On the next restart, all of those writes are queued under the stale `MULTI`, and the EOF handler hits "Revert incomplete MULTI/EXEC transaction" and truncates to `valid_before_multi`. **Every write since the recovery is lost.** If a later real `EXEC` shows up, the partial old transaction and everything in between run as one transaction instead.

::callout{type="warning"}
The data loss only shows up on the restart *after* the recovery, so the current tests won't catch it.

